layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | In the
    strong factory
    | , a Claude Code session called the
    strong coordinator
    | runs in a herdr terminal and does the orchestration: it picks the next package from the
    a(href="/docs/backlog") backlog
    | , starts a worker for it, runs the gates and integrates what passes. Buildyboy is its control tower. The
    | coordinator reads and writes everything through Buildyboy's MCP server, and you follow along on your phone.
  p This page sets up that link, explains the consumption governor, and shows how herdr terminals appear in Buildyboy.

  h2#pieces The three pieces
  dl
    dt Claude Code
    dd The agents: one coordinator and the workers, each with its own subagents.
    dt herdr (hc)
    dd The execution: terminals that stay alive, so a worker can be watched and corrected while it runs.
    dt Buildyboy
    dd The memory and the phone: backlog, usage and the governor, gates, decisions and the daily digest.

  h2#token Step 1 · Create an agent key
  p
    | The coordinator does not use a machine's key. It uses an
    strong agent key
    | , which can only reach the factory's tools, not your sessions' controls or settings, and which you can revoke at
    | any time.
  ol
    li
      | Open
      strong Settings
      | and find the
      strong Factory
      | section.
    li
      | Under
      strong Agent tokens
      | , type a name such as
      code coordinator
      | and create it.
    li
      | Copy the key (it starts with
      code bba_
      | ) right away: it is shown only once. The section also shows a ready
      code claude mcp add
      | line with it filled in.
  p
    | On the server itself,
    code node cli/bb.ts agent-token coordinator
    | does the same and prints the key.

  h2#register Step 2 · Register the MCP server
  p
    | Run this once on the machine where the coordinator will run, with your server's address, your key and the folder
    | where the Buildyboy code is:
  pre
    code
      | claude mcp add buildyboy --scope user -e BUILDYBOY_URL=https://buildyboy.example -e BUILDYBOY_AGENT_TOKEN=bba_… -- node C:\path\to\buildyboy\cli\bb.ts mcp
  p
    | Claude Code then starts
    code bb mcp
    | by itself in every session. Check it with
    code claude mcp list
    | ; inside a session,
    code /mcp
    | shows the buildyboy tools.
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        | The key goes into Claude Code's own configuration file. Treat it like a password, and revoke it in Settings if
        | it leaks; a revoked key stops working at once.

  h2#tools The tools
  p Each tool answers with JSON. A refused call says why, for example "task P3 waits on P1".
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Tool
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") What it does
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code usage_get
          td(data-part="td") The 5-hour and 7-day windows: percent used, when each resets, and how old the data is.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code governor_get
          td(data-part="td") May a new worker start now? See the governor below.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code sessions_list, sessions_get
          td(data-part="td") Status, context, cost and branch of the sessions Buildyboy knows.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code tasks_list, tasks_get
          td(data-part="td") The backlog, or only the packages ready to start; one package with its history, gates and requests.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code tasks_create, tasks_update
          td(data-part="td") Add a package (also from chosen findings), or change its status, branch, cost, prompt version, terminal, used migrations or note.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code tasks_claim
          td(data-part="td") Take the next ready package (or a given one), mark it În lucru and get its port, database and migration range. Refused while the governor says no.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code prompts_render, prompts_lint, prompts_save
          td(data-part="td") Fill the harness template, check it, and save it as a versioned prompt tied to a package.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code gates_record
          td(data-part="td") Record a gate result (G1 to G5) with a summary, findings and screenshots taken from local files.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code gates_run, gates_status
          td(data-part="td") Run a gate on the runner from the repo's recipe and get back only the verdict, summary and findings. The log stays in Buildyboy.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code findings_list, findings_update
          td(data-part="td") Gate findings by package, status and severity; mark them fixed, deferred, for the architect or won't fix.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code decision_ask, decision_get
          td(data-part="td") Ask you something on your phone; read your answer later.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code digest_post
          td(data-part="td") Send the digest now, with an optional note.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code control_get
          td(data-part="td") Your requests to stop a package or revert its integration, so the coordinator can act on them.

  h2#governor The consumption governor
  p
    | Your subscription has a rolling 5-hour window and a weekly 7-day window. The governor reads them from the
    | statusline bridge and answers the coordinator's question: can I start a worker now, or until when should I wait?
  ul
    li
      strong 7-day window at 80% or more:
      | the factory stops entirely until that window resets, so the rest of the week stays for the sessions you run
      | yourself.
    li
      strong 5-hour window at 85% or more:
      | no new workers until it resets; the ones already running finish.
    li
      strong A window missing from the data
      | (for example after a plan change) is ignored.
    li
      strong Data older than 30 minutes, or none at all:
      | no new workers, and the reason says so.
  p
    | The three numbers are in Settings → Factory. The backlog page shows the governor's answer at the top, and
    code GET /api/governor
    | returns it as JSON.

  h2#bookkeeping The factory's bookkeeping
  ul
    li
      strong Rounds:
      | every move into La porți starts a gate round. When a gate fails in the last allowed round (3 by default,
      | Settings → Factory), the package becomes Blocată and you get one decision: another round, another round for
      | one gate only, or stop the package. Moving it back to În lucru with a note allows one more round.
    li
      strong Cost:
      | sessions started with
      code BB_TASK
      | and
      code BB_ROLE
      | (lucrator, qa, poarta, coordonator) are linked to the package, as are its herdr terminal and its job. The
      | package page shows their exact sum, split by role.
    li
      strong Gates on the runner:
      | each repo names its recipe, a JSON file in its main checkout, never in the worker's worktree.
      code gates_run
      | sends one gate to the runner, which runs its steps without a shell and reports the verdict, summary and
      | findings. The log stays on the package page.
    li
      strong Resources:
      code tasks_claim
      | gives each package a port, a database name and a range of migration numbers from the repo's config. When a
      | package is integrated with migrations inside another active package's range, that package and the digest get
      | a warning to renumber.
    li
      strong Findings
      | are kept with a status. A later pass of the same gate marks its blockers and majors fixed; minors stay open
      | until you pick some on the backlog and make a package from them.
    li
      strong When the server is away,
      code bb mcp
      | queues gates_record, tasks_update, digest_post and prompts_save in
      code ~/.buildyboy/outbox.jsonl
      | and replays them once it is back, each exactly once. Reads return the last known answer marked stale, and
      | governor_get is computed from the local statusline sample. With
      code ntfyTopic
      | in
      code ~/.buildyboy/config.json
      | you get one phone message after 10 minutes without the server, and one when it is back.

  h2#herdr herdr terminals
  p
    | When a machine's
    a(href="/docs/runner") runner
    | finds
    code hc
    | , it reports herdr's terminals every 15 seconds, and the
    a(href="/docs/floor") Floor
    | lists them with their status. A terminal is linked to the Claude Code session running inside it, and to its
    | package when it was started for one.
  p
    | The runner looks for
    code hc
    | in this order:
    code hcPath
    | in
    code runner.json
    | , the
    code BUILDYBOY_HC
    | variable, herdr-core's usual install folder on Windows, then your PATH. Set either one to
    code off
    | to turn the integration off.
  p
    | From a package's page you can also start it as an interactive agent in a new herdr terminal, rather than as a
    | one-shot job: see
    a(href="/docs/backlog#launch") Starting a package
    | .

  h2#activate Turning it on for this machine
  p The governor needs usage data, and nothing in Buildyboy edits Claude Code's settings for you. On the machine where the coordinator runs:
  ol
    li
      | Pair the machine and install the plugin, as in
      a(href="/docs/machines") Pairing machines
      | .
    li
      | Add the statusline bridge to
      code ~/.claude/settings.json
      | . This is where the usage windows come from:
      pre
        code
          | "statusLine": { "type": "command", "command": "node C:/path/to/buildyboy/plugin/statusline/bb-statusline.mjs" }
      | If you already have a status line, put its command in the
      code BUILDYBOY_STATUSLINE_NEXT
      | variable and the bridge runs it after itself.
    li
      | Optionally paste the OTEL block from Settings → Snippets into the
      code env
      | section of the same file, for exact costs and tokens.
    li
      | Create an agent key and register the MCP server (steps 1 and 2 above).
    li
      | Run the doctor with the key set. Its
      strong factory
      | block lists exactly what is still missing, with the next step for each:
      pre
        code
          | $env:BUILDYBOY_AGENT_TOKEN='bba_…'; node cli/bb.ts doctor
    li Start an interactive claude session (the status line only runs there), wait a minute, and check that the backlog page shows fresh usage.
